WEBVTT

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:10.180
Every mark on these sheets was made by somebody reading. So what did they keep? Let's find out, with a book short enough to read in thirty seconds.

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:22.980
Treat capitals and lowercase as the same word, even on printed sheets. "The" and "the" go in the same row. Full stops and exclamation marks count as words too.

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:40.520
Now read it two words at a time. Find the row for the first word. The second word goes in a box on that row, with a mark beside it. Then slide along by one, and do it again.

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:54.180
When a different word follows "the", it takes the next empty box. And when a pair comes round again, it gets another mark. By the end, "the fence" has six marks, and "the magpie" five.

00:00:55.310 --> 00:01:02.200
For our model, training means counting pairs. Here's the rest of the book, counted by a computer.

00:01:03.790 --> 00:01:10.660
We can put the book away now. The sheets have counts for each pair of words, and that's what we'll use to write.

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:18.500
And a row of marks is just a row of numbers. The row for "the" says five, six, four, four.

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:31.120
When people talk about a model's "weights", they mean numbers like these, set by the text it read. An LLM's weights aren't tallies of pairs, but they do the same job: predicting what comes next.

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:40.580
We can count a bigger book the same way. This is Frankenstein. Eighty-five thousand words, and seven thousand rows.

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:52.740
A large language model reads trillions of words, and it doesn't keep a box for every pair. But it's training for the same job: guessing what comes next. Yours fits on a desk.

00:01:53.390 --> 00:02:10.960
Three things catch people out. A full stop is a word, and its row says how sentences start. A word that's new to the row goes in the next empty box; never skip one. And say a new word out loud when you start its row, so nobody starts it twice.

00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:16.240
Pick a reader to grab the text. Two words at a time.
